<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="glutathione biosynthesis" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="GLUTATHIONESYN-PWY" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Common Viridiplantae Pathways (CVP) generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are thought to be present in all or most Viridiplantae species including algae. All UPP pathways are automatically imported into databases regardless of Pathologic predictions. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;General Background&lt;/b&gt; Thiols play a major role in the detoxification of stress-inducing factors such as reactive oxygen species, free radicals, peroxides, lipid peroxides, and heavy metals. In mammals, the major thiol is the tripeptide |FRAME: GLUTATHIONE| (&amp;gamma;-Glu-Cys-Gly, known as GSH). Toxins are conjugated to GSH in the liver by a dedicated enzyme, resulting in a |FRAME: S-Substituted-L-Cysteines &quot;cysteine-toxin conjugate&quot;|. The conjugate is acetylated in the kidney to form |FRAME: Mercapturates|, and is then excreted |CITS: [8145281]| (see |FRAME: PWY-4061|). In addition to oxidative stress management, low molecular weight thiols have been found to be involved in critical cellular processes including DNA synthesis and formaldehyde reduction |CITS: [Patel98]|. Gluathione is the major thiol not only in eukaryotic organisms but also in Gram negative bacteria and in the majority of Gram positive bacteria. Pathogenic bacteria live in a particularly hostile environment, in which the host deliberately generates toxins, including reactive oxygen species, to destroy the invading organism, thus thiols are particularly important for the survival of these organisms. Despite the ubiquity of GSH, it is not universal. In many organisms, including some eukaryotes, other thiols have taken its role. Some of these thiols are |FRAME: CoM|, |FRAME: TRYPANOTHIONE|, |FRAME: Ergothioneine|, |FRAME: CPD1G-2| and |FRAME: CPD-17028|. &lt;b&gt;About This Pathway&lt;/b&gt; The synthesis of GSH from its three amino acid precursors L-glutamate, L-cysteine and glycine takes place in the cytosol. GSH is synthesized in all mammalian cells, many plants, and most Gram negative bacteria. In mammals, the liver is a major site of biosynthesis. GSH is synthesized in a two-step reaction, catalyzed by |FRAME: EC-6.3.2.2| and |FRAME: EC-6.3.2.3|, requiring a total of two moles of ATP per mole of GSH. This ATP-dependent coupling of amino acid monomers and elongating peptides by soluble enzymes is very unusual, as oligopeptides are usually formed either by ribosomes or by |FRAME: Non-ribosomal-peptide-synthetases &quot;nonribosomal peptide synthases&quot;|. A similar mechanism is also found in the |FRAME: Acetylmuramoyl-Pentapeptide-Biosynthesis &quot;formation of the bacterial cell wall pentapeptide&quot;|. The first step in glutathione biosynthesis is controlled by negative feedback from its end product, GSH. However, feedback inhibition can be partially prevented by an excess of glutamate that blocks the regulatory site on the enzyme |CITS: [6151157][6137189]|. When GSH is consumed and feedback inhibition is lost, the availability of cysteine as a precursor can become the limiting factor |CITS: [9755286]|. &lt;b&gt;In plants&lt;/b&gt; Glutathione acts in plants as an antioxidant that counteracts reactive oxygen species thus reducing oxidative stress |CITS: [NOCTOR98]|. as in animals, it is a major player in detoxifying xenobiotics |CITS: [16665956]| by forming less reactive or storage conjugates catalyzed by GHS S-transferases |CITS: [15012285]|. Glutathione has also been reported to detoxify air pollutants such as ozone and sulfur dioxide in palnts |CITS: [16668420]|. GHS appears to be an important storage form of reduced sulfur in plants, regulating inter-organ sulfur allocation. Its biosynthesis seems to be coupled with the sulfate assimilation pathway (see |FRAME:SULFMETII-PWY|) |CITS: [12481075]|. As a precursor for |FRAME: Poly-Gamma-Glutamylcysteine-Glycines phytochelatin|, the principal metal-binding agent in plants, glutathione is considered a vital component for the detoxification of heavy metals |CITS: [GRILL85]| |CITS: [16594069][11402187]|. The biosynthesis of glutathione is universally catalyzed in all organisms by two enzymes, glutamate-cysteine ligase |CITS: [May94][15180996]| regarded as the rate-limiting step of the pathway |CITS: [1112810]| and glutathione synthetase |CITS: [8612643][8914526][15302873]|. The regulation of the plant enzymes is unlike that reported from other eukaryotes. For the glutamate-cysteine ligase a conformational change has been observed as means to modify enzyme activities in response to redox conditions. That differs from mammalian enzymes that regulate activity through a regulatory subunit of the enzyme |CITS: [15180996]|.</Description>
